{
  "id": "valid-perfect-square",
  "questionId": "valid-perfect-square",
  "slug": "valid-perfect-square",
  "title": "Valid Perfect Square",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Binary search",
  "patterns": [
    "binary search"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "you're really asking: does some integer i in a bounded range square to exactly num?",
    "i * i grows monotonically as i grows, so the candidates are effectively sorted by their square",
    "binary search on i over [0, num]: compare mid * mid to num and shrink the range toward equality",
    "if the search ends without ever finding i * i == num exactly, the answer is false - don't just check the final boundary loosely"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "using a built-in sqrt and comparing with floating-point equality, which can be wrong due to precision errors",
    "overflow computing mid * mid for large num in fixed-width integer languages",
    "forgetting to check num = 0 and num = 1 as valid perfect squares"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a non-negative integer `num`.\n\n**Return true if `num` is a perfect square (the square of some integer), or false otherwise.**\n\nYou may not use a built-in square root function.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= num <= 2^31 - 1"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "num = 0 (0 * 0 = 0, a perfect square)",
      "num = 1 (trivially a perfect square)",
      "a large num just below or just above a perfect square"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
